# BUILD-AND-WATCH.md §4.3. `--watch` refuses two combinations, and both are
# refused at argument parsing with exit 2 — the thing you asked *with* is wrong,
# and no repository had to be opened to know it.
#
# This is also where the non-terminal refusal is *provable*: every harness in
# this repository pipes stdout, so a case here is a watch loop asked for in
# exactly the situation the check exists to catch. The loop itself is driven in
# `cli/tests/build/watch.rs`, headlessly, with the terminal flag injected.
#
# The order the checks are written in is part of the contract: a flag
# combination is a mistake a person can fix by reading, so it is reported ahead
# of the terminal check rather than behind it. Without that, both of these would
# say "needs a terminal" and the real mistake would be invisible.
#
# There was a third, `--accept`, and the last run below is what is left of it.
# That flag rewrote the golden files a suite declared in `test { data }`; the
# field is retired and the flag went with it, so the answer is no longer a
# refusal of a combination but a retired flag saying what replaced it — which is
# a different sentence and is worth pinning, because the alternative is
# `nearest` offering somebody `--explain`.
#
# Schema: the repository-case manifest, cli/tests/harness/case.rs. Textproto,
# but not a Buri file: only repo/ below is a Buri repository.

doc: "--watch refuses --force and a stdout that is not a terminal, belongs to `test` alone, and a retired flag says what replaced it"

# The suite passes without `--watch`, so every refusal below is about the flag
# rather than about the repository.
run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/money"]
  exit: 0
}

# `--force` turns every cache hit into a run, and the cache is the whole of what
# makes the loop cheap.
run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/money", "--watch", "--force"]
  exit: 2
  golden: "force.txt"
  stream: ERR
}

# And the one this case can only make in a harness: stdout is a pipe.
run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/money", "--watch"]
  exit: 2
  golden: "not_a_terminal.txt"
  stream: ERR
}

# `--watch` belongs to `buri test` and to nothing else, and the command table is
# what enforces that: no code in `build` had to know the flag exists.
run {
  args: ["build", "//lib/money", "--watch"]
  exit: 2
  golden: "wrong_command.txt"
  stream: ERR
}

# A flag that was taken away is not a flag that never existed.
run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/money", "--accept"]
  exit: 2
  golden: "retired_accept.txt"
  stream: ERR
}
